From 25771a1be3afb6052ed749630d139ae2764dee7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Fink <7587613+deadpoets@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:17:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] runtime, runtime/secret: support windows/amd64

Enable secret.Do on windows/amd64. The portable parts of the runtime
support (secret counter, stack erasure, secret heap specials, the
preemption refusal in isAsyncSafePoint and the register erasure on
stack switches) already build for any GOOS; only the build tags and
the signal-stack handling were Linux-specific.

Windows has no signal stacks: mpreinit does not allocate mp.gsignal,
and exceptions are dispatched on the faulting thread's current stack.
A fault inside Do leaves the kernel-written CONTEXT and the handler's
register spills on the goroutine stack below SP, inside the range that
secret_eraseSecrets clears, and nothing register-derived on g0. Split
the signal-stack functions into secret_signal_unix.go and a Windows
file where they are no-ops, and add a test-only hook returning the g0
stacks so the Windows test can check them.

TestExceptionStacks is the Windows counterpart of TestSignalStacks:
fault inside Do, then check the goroutine stack and the committed
pages of every g0 stack. TestCore has no Windows counterpart yet.

Updates #81796

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
